The Korean War, which was fought between 1950 and 1953, was a brutal conflict that resulted in the deaths of millions of people and the destruction of a significant portion of the Korean Peninsula. While the war was fought between North Korea, which was supported by China, and South Korea, which was supported by the United States and other members of the United Nations, many South Korean experts argue that the war was solely initiated by South Korea and that the North's involvement was purely defensive.

According to these experts, South Korea was the one that wanted to unify the Korean Peninsula under its own government and it was South Korea that crossed the 38th parallel to initiate a war against the North. The North, on the other hand, only responded to this aggression and its actions were limited to a defensive nature. This argument is supported by the fact that South Korea had been actively preparing for war against the North since the end of the Second World War and had even signed several treaties with other countries, including the United States, to facilitate the possibility of war.

